[node name="Rummy Help Text" type="RichTextLabel" parent="PanelContainer/Control/TabContainer/Rummy 300 Rules"]
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_horizontal = 3
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "
[b]Rummy 300[/b] is a card game played over a number of rounds. The goal is to be the first player to reach 300 points.

Each round begins with each player being dealt a set number of cards (10 with two players, 8 with three or 7 with four).
The remaining cards are placed face-down to form the [i]deck[/i], and the top card is turned over to begin the face-up [i]discard pile[/i].

At the beginning of each player's turn, they must draw from either the deck or the discard pile. You may only pick up a single card from the deck, but may pick up any number of cards from the discard pile; if you pick up multiple cards from the discard pile, you must immediately use the bottommost picked up card.

During your turn, you may place down [i]melds[/i], or lay off cards onto existing melds.
Laying off is adding a connected card to an existing placed-down meld. You may only lay off once you have melded at least once.
If you are able to lay off, you may lay off any number of times during your turn.
In this interface, you lay off by dragging the card from your hand to the meld.

[font_size=18][b][i]Melds[/i][/b][/font_size]
Melds are groups of cards which you can place down, removing them from your hand. The first player to end a turn with no cards left wins the round. 
There are two types of melds: [b]Runs[/b] and [b]Sets[/b].
[b]Sets[/b] are groupings of three or more cards of any [i]suit[/i] with the same [i]rank[/i]. Eg: [img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of spades.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of clubs.tres[/img]
[b]Runs[/b] are groupings of three or more cards of the same [i]suit[/i] and consecutive [i]rank[/i]. Eg: [img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/two of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/four of hearts.tres[/img]
Aces are low for the purpose of runs.

You may only meld once per turn unless you are [i]rummying[/i].

[font_size=18][b][i]Rummying[/i][/b][/font_size]
Rummying is getting rid of all your cards in one go. While rummying, you may meld multiple times in the same turn. You may only rummy if you have not melded previously in the round, and you will have no cards left at the end of the turn (either by using all your cards or discarding your last card). When you win by rummying, the score you get is doubled.

[font_size=18][b][i]Discarding[/i][/b][/font_size]
The turn ends when you discard a card, which goes to the top of the discard pile. If you picked up from the discard pile, you may not discard the topmost picked up card on the same turn unless it is the last card remaining in your hand.

[font_size=18][b][i]Score[/i][/b][/font_size]
When a player goes out, the scores of all the cards remaining in all other player's hands are totalled, and this is added to the winner's score. If they won by rummying, this is doubled.
Numbered cards are worth their number's worth of points, Aces are worth 1 and face cards are worth 10."
fit_content = true
script = ExtResource("4_416vn")
markdown_text = "
**Rummy 300** is a card game played over a number of rounds. The goal is to be the first player to reach 300 points.

Each round begins with each player being dealt a set number of cards (10 with two players, 8 with three or 7 with four).
The remaining cards are placed face-down to form the *deck*, and the top card is turned over to begin the face-up *discard pile*.

At the beginning of each player's turn, they must draw from either the deck or the discard pile. You may only pick up a single card from the deck, but may pick up any number of cards from the discard pile; if you pick up multiple cards from the discard pile, you must immediately use the bottommost picked up card.

During your turn, you may place down *melds*, or lay off cards onto existing melds.
Laying off is adding a connected card to an existing placed-down meld. You may only lay off once you have melded at least once.
If you are able to lay off, you may lay off any number of times during your turn.
In this interface, you lay off by dragging the card from your hand to the meld.

## Melds
Melds are groups of cards which you can place down, removing them from your hand. The first player to end a turn with no cards left wins the round. 
There are two types of melds: **Runs** and **Sets**.
**Sets** are groupings of three or more cards of any *suit* with the same *rank*. Eg: [img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of spades.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of clubs.tres[/img]
**Runs** are groupings of three or more cards of the same *suit* and consecutive *rank*. Eg: [img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/two of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/three of hearts.tres[/img][img height=20]res://assets/textures/split out mini card textures/four of hearts.tres[/img]
Aces are low for the purpose of runs.

You may only meld once per turn unless you are *rummying*.

## Rummying
Rummying is getting rid of all your cards in one go. While rummying, you may meld multiple times in the same turn. You may only rummy if you have not melded previously in the round, and you will have no cards left at the end of the turn (either by using all your cards or discarding your last card). When you win by rummying, the score you get is doubled.

## Discarding
The turn ends when you discard a card, which goes to the top of the discard pile. If you picked up from the discard pile, you may not discard the topmost picked up card on the same turn unless it is the last card remaining in your hand.

## Score
When a player goes out, the scores of all the cards remaining in all other player's hands are totalled, and this is added to the winner's score. If they won by rummying, this is doubled.
Numbered cards are worth their number's worth of points, Aces are worth 1 and face cards are worth 10."
